{"name":"Indian Spice","hex":"#ae8845","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"indian","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative letter-case form of Indian (“indigene”)."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Alternative letter-case form of Indian (“indigenous”)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/indian"},{"term":"spice","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Aromatic or pungent plant matter (usually dried) used to season or flavor food.","The quality of being spicy."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To add spice or spices to; season.","To spice up."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/spice"}],"entries":[{"title":"List of Indian spices","blocks":[{"text":"Indian spices include a variety of spices grown across the Indian subcontinent. With different climates in different parts of the country, India produces a variety of spices, many of which are native to the subcontinent. Others were imported from similar climates and have since been cultivated locally for centuries. Pepper, turmeric, cardamom, and cumin are some examples of Indian spices."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/82/Common_Indian_spices.jpg/330px-Common_Indian_spices.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_Indian_spices","wikidata":"Q3595160"},{"title":"Spice","blocks":[{"text":"In the culinary arts, a spice is a seed, fruit, root, bark, or other plant substance primarily used for flavouring or colouring food. Spices are distinguished from herbs, which are the leaves, flowers, or stems of plants used for flavouring or as a garnish. Spices and herbs are both seasonings. \nSpices are sometimes used in medicine, religious rituals, cosmetics, or perfume production. They are usually classified into spices, spice seeds, and herbal categories. For example, vanilla is commonly used as an ingredient in fragrance manufacturing. Plant-based sweeteners such as sugar are not considered spices."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Spices1.jpg/330px-Spices1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spice","wikidata":"Q42527"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Spices_in_an_Indian_market.jpg/500px-Spices_in_an_Indian_market.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Spices_in_an_Indian_market.jpg/500px-Spices_in_an_Indian_market.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Spices at a Goa market","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASpices_in_an_Indian_market.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Indian_spices_with_labels_%28garam_masala_components%29_%2849684333301%29.jpg/500px-Indian_spices_with_labels_%28garam_masala_components%29_%2849684333301%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Indian_spices_with_labels_%28garam_masala_components%29_%2849684333301%29.jpg/1280px-Indian_spices_with_labels_%28garam_masala_components%29_%2849684333301%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Indian_spices_with_labels_%28garam_masala_components%29_%2849684333301%29.jpg/500px-Indian_spices_with_labels_%28garam_masala_components%29_%2849684333301%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Indian spices with labels (garam masala components)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIndian_spices_with_labels_(garam_masala_components)_(49684333301).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/45/Dried_Mango_Slices.JPG/500px-Dried_Mang